Dialogue: 3,0:00:01.70,0:00:05.20,Card,,0,0,0,,{\an8\pos(540,132)\fad(180,220)}“LiteLLM was compromised
Dialogue: 3,0:00:01.70,0:00:05.20,Card,,0,0,0,,{\an8\pos(540,196)\fad(180,220)}through a stolen CLI
Dialogue: 3,0:00:01.70,0:00:05.20,Card,,0,0,0,,{\an8\pos(540,260)\fad(180,220)}token”
Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:01.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,The attack vector today
Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.60,0:00:03.86,Caption,,0,0,0,,though, is more prolific
Dialogue: 0,0:00:03.86,0:00:04.78,Caption,,0,0,0,,than ever of these
Dialogue: 0,0:00:04.78,0:00:05.50,Caption,,0,0,0,,supply chain attacks
Dialogue: 0,0:00:05.50,0:00:06.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,and we saw that with
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.90,0:00:08.30,Caption,,0,0,0,,LiteLLM today
Dialogue: 0,0:00:08.30,0:00:09.22,Caption,,0,0,0,,which was pretty
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.22,0:00:09.52,Caption,,0,0,0,,sophisticated.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.52,0:00:11.13,Caption,,0,0,0,,They compromised
Dialogue: 0,0:00:11.13,0:00:12.53,Caption,,0,0,0,,multiple different core
Dialogue: 0,0:00:12.53,0:00:13.35,Caption,,0,0,0,,contributors to the
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.35,0:00:15.41,Caption,,0,0,0,,project by basically
Dialogue: 0,0:00:15.41,0:00:17.17,Caption,,0,0,0,,getting access to an
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.17,0:00:19.46,Caption,,0,0,0,,OAuth CLI token
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.46,0:00:20.46,Caption,,0,0,0,,using that to create a
Dialogue: 0,0:00:20.46,0:00:21.74,Caption,,0,0,0,,new release.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:21.74,0:00:22.94,Caption,,0,0,0,,and then even like
Dialogue: 0,0:00:22.94,0:00:23.94,Caption,,0,0,0,,hacking another user to
Dialogue: 0,0:00:23.94,0:00:25.12,Caption,,0,0,0,,approve the release
